                %li
                  %a{:href => "#ReferencesInDependentTypeTheory"} In dependent type theory
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            It is common in informal language to speak of mathematical objects “equipped with
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extra+structure"} extra structure
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebras+over+a+Lawvere+theory"} algebras over a Lawvere theory
            in
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
            equipped with the structure of certain algebraic operations. For instance a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi G
                  %mo ,
                  %mi e
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (G, e, {\cdot})
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            equipped with a binary operation
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mo :
                    %mi G
                    %mo ×
                    %mi G
                    %mo →
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {\cdot} : G \times G \to G
          %p
            One may formalize the notion of structure using the language of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stuff%2C+structure%2C+property"} stuff, structure, property
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
            in some
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            are objects in some category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %em equipped with extra structure
            if there is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi D
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p \colon D \to C
            such that
          %ul
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
              is a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/faithful+functor"} faithful functor
          %p#InCategoryTheory
            Depending on author and situation, more properties are required of this functor (
            %a{:href => "#Ehresmann57"}> Ehresmann 57
            ,
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Ehresmann65"} Ehresmann 65
            = succeed "):" do
              %a{:href => "#AdamekRosickyVitale"} Adamek-Rosicky-Vitale 09, remark 13.18
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
                is an
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/amnestic+functor"} amnestic functor
                (
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
                \-vertical
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphisms"} isomorphisms
                are
                = succeed ")," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identities"} identities
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
                is an
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isofibration"} isofibration
                (isomorphisms can be lifted along
                = succeed ")." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi p
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
          %p#StrictStructure
            However, notice that these two conditions violate the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principle+of+equivalence"} principle of equivalence
            for
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories"} categories
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+categories"} strict categories
            one might hence refer to these conditions as expressing “strict extra structure”.
          %h2#notions_of_structure Notions of structure
          %p
            A special class of examples of this is the notion of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structure+in+model+theory"} structure in model theory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            that describes the constants, functions (say operations) and relations with which we want to equip sets, and then sets equipped with those operations and relations are called
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structure+in+model+theory"} structures
            for that language. (Equivalently one might say “sets with
            = succeed "-structure\u201D." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            = succeed "-structure\u201D" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            = succeed "-structure\u201D.)" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/faithful+functor"} faithful functor
            from
            = succeed "-structures" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
          %p
            We instead say
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model"} model
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory"} theory
            when we restrict to those structures which satisfy the axioms of a theory (in other words, satisfy
            %em properties
            specified by the axioms). In this case there is a full and faithful functor from the category of models of a theory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            to the category of structures of the underlying language
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L(T)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Mod
                    %mi T
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi Struct
                    %mrow
                      %mi L
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi T
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Mod_T \to Struct_{L(T)} \to Set
          %p is again faithful.
          .num_remark
            %h6#remarks Remarks
            %p Thus, the English word “structure” is used in several slightly differing mathematical senses.
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  Within category theory itself, “structure” can function as a kind of mass noun, as in a phrase like “forgetting structure”. Here it refers to data comprising operations, relations, constants, and
                  %em also properties
                  borne by models of a theory or relative theory, considered abstractly (for example, the functor
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Grp
                        %mo →
                        %mi Set
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Grp \to Set
                  which forgets group structure, or the functor
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Ring
                        %mo →
                        %mi Ab
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ring \to Ab
                  which forgets multiplicative structure). On the other hand, it can also operate in the singular, where one says for example “a topological group is a topological space equipped with a group structure, such that…”
              %li
                %p
                  In model theory, however, the term
                  %em structure
                  is not a mass noun; it refers to a
                  %em particular
                  set (or “structures” for a family of sets) together with functions, relations, and elements that interpret the symbols of operations, predicates, and constants of a
                  = succeed "." do
                    %em language
                  = succeed "," do
                    %em theory
                  %em satisfied
                  by the structure is called a
                  %em model
                  of the theory. Thus, in summary, the category theorist might refer to “the structure of a group” as consisting of a multiplication, a unit, etc., satisfying group axioms, while the model theorist would say that each particular group (like
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi ℤ
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}
          %h2#examples Examples
          %p There are gazillions of examples of objects equipped with extra structure. The most familiar is maybe
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+structure"}> algebraic structure
              \.
          %p
            Generally the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/forgetful+functor"} forgetful functor
            from a category of algebras over an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+theory"} algebraic theory
            down to the base category exhibits the equipment with the corresponding algebraic structure.
          %h2#InDependentTypeTheory Structures in dependent type theory
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
            the notion of “mathematical structure” and/or
            %em data structure
            on a base
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi Type
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B \,\colon\, Type
            is given by iterated
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+pairs"} dependent pairings
            (hence forming “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+telescopes"}> type telescopes
            ” also called “records” in
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Coq"} Coq
            = succeed "-dependent" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
          %p
            The following shows some examples, using the notation for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+pairs"} dependent pairs
            from
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+functions+and+dependent+pairs+--+table"} here
          %p#StructureIdentityPrinciple
            Via the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extension+%28semantics%29"} extensionality
            principles for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+pairs"} dependent pairs
            (
            %a{:href => "dependent+sum+type#ExtensionalityPrinciple"}> here
            ) and for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+functions"} dependent functions
            (
            %a{:href => "function+extensionality#StatementForDependentFunctions"}> here
            ) such type theoretic structure automatically obey the
            = succeed ":" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structure+identity+principle"} structure identity principle
          %p
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/DependentPairExtensionality-230121.jpg", :width => "600"}/
          %p
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/DependentFunctionExtensionality-230121.jpg", :width => "600"}/
          %p
            In that any equivalence/identification between pairs of data of the following types are isomorphisms in the sense of bijective
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homomorphisms"} homomorphisms
          %h3#lensed_data_structure Lensed data structure
          %p
            To say that a given data (base) type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B \,\colon\, Set
            is
          %ol
            %li
              %p equipped with
              %ol
                %li
                  %p
                    a
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function+type"} function
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi read
                            %mi D
                          %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                          %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                          %mi B
                          %mo →
                          %mi D
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} read_D \,\colon\, B \to D
                    reading out
                    = succeed "-data;" do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mi D
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
                %li
                  %p
                    a
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function+type"} function
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi write
                            %mi D
                          %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                          %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                          %mi D
                          %mo ×
                          %mi B
                          %mo →
                          %mi B
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} write_D \,\colon\, D \times B \to B
                    (over-)writing
                    = succeed "-data;" do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mi D
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            %li
              %p
                such that this does behave as expected, namely as a
                %a{:href => "lens+in+computer+science#LensesAreCostateCoalgebras"} well-behaved
                = succeed "-" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi D
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
                = succeed "-structure" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lens+%28in+computer+science%29"} lens
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
          %p
            means to declare it to be of the following iterated
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+pair"} dependent pair
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/telescope"} telescope
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type"} type
          %p
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/WellBehavedLensDataStructure-230130.jpg", :width => "740"}/
          %h3#GroupDataStructure Group data structure
          %p
            The
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+pair"} dependent pair
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/telescope"} telescope
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type"} type
            declaration of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+objects"} group structure
          %p
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/GroupDataType-230130.jpg", :width => "710"}/
          %p
            Further restriction to
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+groups"} abelian groups
          %p
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/AbelianGroupData-230129.jpg", :width => "620"}/
          %p
            Via the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structure+identity+principle"} structure identity principle
            (
            %a{:href => "#StructureIdentityPrinciple"}> above
            ), the
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identification+type"} identitifications
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+equivalence"} equivalences
            between such group data types are indeed bijective
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+homomorphisms"} group homomorphisms
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphisms"} isomorphisms
          %p
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/GroupDataIsomorphism-230131.jpg", :width => "820"}/
          %p
            The structure of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subgroups"} subgroups
            of a given group structure:
          %p
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/SubgroupDataStructure-230201.jpg", :width => "900"}/
          %p
            and their
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/underlying"} underlying
            abstract group structure:
          %p
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/UnderlyingGroupOfSubgroupStructure-230201.jpg", :width => "900"}/
          %h3#GroupActionStructure Group action structure
          %p
            The
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+pair"} dependent pair
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/telescope"} telescope
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type"} type
            declaration of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+actions"} group actions
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/G-sets"}> G-sets
            ):
          %p
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/GroupActionDataStructure-230220.jpg", :width => "900"}/
          %p
            Specialization to
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/torsors"} torsors
          %p
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/TorsorDataStructure-230220.jpg", :width => "900"}/
          %h3#RingDataStructure Ring data structure
          %p
            The
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+pair"} dependent pair
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/telescope"} telescope
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type"} type
            declaration of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unital+ring"} unital
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring+objects"} ring structure
          %p
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/RingDataStruture-230129.jpg", :width => "740"}/
          %p
            For examples see at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/numbers"} numbers
            –
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/number#InDependentTypeTheory"} In dependent type theory
          %h3#ModuleDataStructure Module data structure
          %p
            Given a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unital+ring"} unital
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring"} ring
            type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi Ring
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R \colon Ring
            (as
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "#RingDataStructure"} above
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/module+objects"} module objects
            is the following
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+pair"} dependent pair
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/telescope"} telescope
          %p
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/ModuleDataStructure-230129.jpg", :width => "740"}/
          %h2#HigherStructuresInHoTT Higher structures in homotopy type theory
          %p
            The
            %a{:href => "#InDependentTypeTheory"} above
            examples of structures formulated in dependent type theory all had data
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/h-set"} sets
            as their base, which is the classical situation. But in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
            with untruncated
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identification+types"} identification types
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type"} homotopy type
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+structures"} higher structures
          %h3#delooping Delooping
          %p
            Given a set-based
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+object"} group structure
            (as
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "#GroupDataStructure"} above
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/delooping"} delooping
            structure is
          %p (…)
          %h3#higher_delooping Higher delooping
          %p
            More generally, one may consider delooping of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-groups"}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                \-groups
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pointed+object"}
              pointed
            types (cf.
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "delooping#Wärn23"} Wärn (2023, §2)
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "delooping#BCFR23"} BCFR23
          %p
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/PointedHigherDeloopingStructure-230131.jpg", :width => "700"}/
          %p
            Similarly there is
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/iterated+loop+space"} iterated delooping structure
          %p
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/PointedIteratedDeloopingStructure-230131.jpg", :width => "740"}/
          %h2#related_entries Related entries
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Evident as the notion of
                %em mathematical structure
                may seem these days, it was at least not made explicit until the middle of the 20th century. Then it was the influence of the
                = succeed "-project" do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bourbaki"} Bourbaki
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
                which made the notion explicit and finally led to the above formalization.
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functions"} functions
                that preserves extra structure are called
                = succeed ";" do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homomorphisms"} homomorphisms
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relations"} relations
                that preserve extra structure are called
                = succeed "_" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logical+relations"} logical relations
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Birkhoff%27s+HSP+theorem"} Birkhoff's HSP theorem
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structuralism"}> structuralism
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structure+identity+principle"} structure identity principle
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exceptional+structure"} exceptional structure
          %h2#references References
          %h3#general General
          %p
            On the history of the notion of “mathematical structure” with some focus on what
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bourbaki"} Bourbaki
            did and did not contribute to the subject:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Leo+Corry"}> Leo Corry
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Mathematical Structures from Hilbert to Bourbaki: The Evolution of an Image of Mathematics
                %em Changing Images of Mathematics in History. From the French Revolution to the new Millenium
                Harwood Academic Publishers (2001) 167-186 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://www.routledge.com/Changing-Images-in-Mathematics-From-the-French-Revolution-to-the-New-Millennium/Bottazini-Dalmedico/p/book/9780415868273"}> ISBN:9780415868273
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.leocorry.com/_files/ugd/e6fef7_15c7077b08ec4f249fb40eb961256ced.pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Leo+Corry"}> Leo Corry
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Modern Algebra and the Rise of Mathematical Structures
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-0348-7917-0"} doi:10.1007/978-3-0348-7917-0
          %p See also
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Wikipedia,
                %em
                  %a{:href => "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mathematical_structure"} Mathematical structure
            %li
              %p
                Wikipedia,
                %em
                  %a{:href => "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Structure_(mathematical_logic)"} Structure_(mathematical_logic)
          %p
            Early proposal to grasp the notion of mathematical structures via
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            (specifically via
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/forgetful+functors"} forgetful functors
            between
            = succeed "):" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoids"} groupoids
          %ul
            %li#Ehresmann57
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Charles+Ehresmann"}> Charles Ehresmann
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Gattungen in Lokalen Strukturen
                %strong 60
                (1958) 49-77 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://eudml.org/doc/146434"}> dml:146434
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li#Ehresmann65
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Charles+Ehresmann"}> Charles Ehresmann
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Catégories et Structures
                %strong 6
                (1964) 1-31 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "http://www.numdam.org/item?id=SE_1964__6__A8_0"}> numdam:SE_1964__6__A8_0
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://eudml.org/doc/112200"} dml:112200
          %p
            For modern accounts on mathematical structures via
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+algebra"} categorical algebra
            see also at
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+theory"} algebraic theory
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad"} monad
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sketch"} sketch
          %h3#ReferencesInDependentTypeTheory In dependent type theory
          %p
            Discussion of mathematical structures via
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
          %p
            the general idea of representing mathematical structures as
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+telescopes"} type telescopes
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jeffery+Zucker"}> Jeffery Zucker
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Formalization of Classical Mathematics in Automath
                %strong 249
                (1975) 135-145 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://www.win.tue.nl/automath/archive/webversion/aut042/aut042.html"}> web
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.win.tue.nl/automath/archive/pdf/aut042.pdf"} pdf
              %p
                also in: Studies in Logic and the Foundations of Mathematics
                %strong 133
                (1994) 127-139 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/S0049-237X(08)70202-7"}> doi:10.1016/S0049-237X(08)70202-7
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nicolaas+de+Bruijn"}> Nicolaas de Bruijn
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Telescopic mappings in typed lambda calculus
                %strong 91
                2 (1991) 189-204 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/0890-5401(91)90066-B"}> doi:10.1016/0890-5401(91)90066-B
                \&amp;rbrack;
          %p
            and with emphasis of the notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
            between such structures:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+McAllester"}> David McAllester
              ,
              %em Dependent Type Theory as Related to the Bourbaki Notions of Structure and Isomorphism
              \&amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2104.08958"}> arXiv:2104.08958
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p
            in
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Coq"} Coq
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Herman+Geuvers"}> Herman Geuvers
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Randy+Pollack"} Randy Pollack
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Freek+Wiedijk"} Freek Wiedijk
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jan+Zwanenburg"} Jan Zwanenburg
                = succeed "," do
                  %em A Constructive Algebraic Hierarchy in Coq
                %strong 34
                4 (2002) 271-286 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1006/jsco.2002.0552"}> doi:10.1006/jsco.2002.0552
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.cs.ru.nl/~herman/PUBS/JSC2002-GeuversPollackWiedijkZwanenburg-alghier1.pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                Claudio Sacerdoti Coen, Enrico Tassi,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Working with Mathematical Structures in Type Theory
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Types for Proofs and Programs. TYPES 2007
                %strong 4941
                Springer (2008) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-540-68103-8_11"}> doi:10.1007/978-3-540-68103-8_11
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.cs.unibo.it/~sacerdot/PAPERS/types07.pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                François Garillot,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Georges+Gonthier"} Georges Gonthier
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Packaging Mathematical Structures
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Theorem Proving in Higher Order Logics. TPHOLs 2009
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong 5674
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-642-03359-9_23"} doi:10.1007/978-3-642-03359-9_23
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bas+Spitters"}> Bas Spitters
                , Eelis van der Wegen
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Type classes for mathematics in type theory
                %strong 21
                4 “Interactive Theorem Proving and the Formalisation of Mathematics” (2011) 795-825 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1017/S0960129511000119"}> doi:10.1017/S0960129511000119
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1102.1323"} arXiv:1102.1323
              %blockquote
                %p
                  (via
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/ufias2012/published/Type+classes"} Type classes
            %li
              %p
                Kazuhiko Sakaguchi,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Validating Mathematical Structures
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Automated Reasoning. IJCAR 2020
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong 12167
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-030-51054-1_8"} doi:10.1007/978-3-030-51054-1_8
          %p
            in
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lean"} Lean
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib-overview.html"}> The mathlib Community
              , §4 in:
              = succeed "," do
                %em The Lean mathematical library
              %em CPP 2020: Proceedings of the 9th ACM SIGPLAN International Conference on Certified Programs and Proofs
              (2020) 367–381 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1910.09336"}> arXiv:1910.09336
              ,
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1145/3372885.3373824"} doi:10.1145/3372885.3373824
          %p
            Discussion of classes of mathematical structures in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/univalence"} univalent
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/univalent+foundations+of+mathematics"}> univalent foundations of mathematics
            ) which satisfy a
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structure+identity+principle"} structure identity principle
          %ul
            %li#CoquandDanielsson13
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thierry+Coquand"}> Thierry Coquand
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nils+Anders+Danielsson"} Nils Anders Danielsson
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Isomorphism is equality
                %strong 24
                4 (2013) 1105-1120 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.indag.2013.09.002"}> doi:10.1016/j.indag.2013.09.002
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li#UFP13
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Univalent+Foundations+Project"}> Univalent Foundations Project
                , §9.8 but also §2 of:
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Homotopy+Type+Theory+--+Univalent+Foundations+of+Mathematics"} Homotopy Type Theory – Univalent Foundations of Mathematics
                (2013) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "http://homotopytypetheory.org/book/"}> web
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "http://hottheory.files.wordpress.com/2013/03/hott-online-323-g28e4374.pdf"} pdf
            %li{:id => "Escardó19"}
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mart%C3%ADn+Escard%C3%B3"}> Martín Escardó
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a{:href => "https://www.cs.bham.ac.uk/~mhe/HoTT-UF-in-Agda-Lecture-Notes/HoTT-UF-Agda.html#sns"} A structure identity principle for a standard notion of structure
                %em Introduction to Univalent Foundations of Mathematics with Agda
                \&amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1911.00580"}> arXiv:1911.00580
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.cs.bham.ac.uk/~mhe/HoTT-UF-in-Agda-Lecture-Notes/HoTT-UF-Agda.html"} webpage
              %blockquote
                %p
                  (formalized in the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Agda"} Agda
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proof+assistant"} proof assistant
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Benedikt+Ahrens"}> Benedikt Ahrens
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Paige+Randall+North"} Paige Randall North
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Shulman"} Michael Shulman
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dimitris+Tsementzis"} Dimitris Tsementzis
                = succeed "," do
                  %em A Higher Structure Identity Principle
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2004.06572"} arXiv:2004.06572
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1145/3373718.3394755"} doi:10.1145/3373718.3394755
          %p
            Discussion in the context of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/philosophy"} philosophy
            of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structuralism"} structuralism
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dimitris+Tsementzis"}> Dimitris Tsementzis
              , §5.1 in:
              = succeed "," do
                %em Univalent foundations as structuralist foundations
              %strong 194
              9 (2017) 3583–3617 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://www.jstor.org/stable/26748765"}> jstor:26748765
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/s11229-016-1109-x"} doi:10.1007/s11229-016-1109-x
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://core.ac.uk/download/pdf/157866891.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            Expressions towards the notion that, thereby the notions of
            %em mathematical structures
            are
            %em data structures
            may be understood to coincide (just along the lines of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/types"} types
            being
            = succeed "):" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/data+types"} data types
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thomas+Kehrenberg"}> Thomas Kehrenberg
              ,
              %em Basic building blocks of dependent type theory
              (Dec. 2022) &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://www.lesswrong.com/posts/ccbsYSpTcTqXwukH8/basic-building-blocks-of-dependent-type-theory"}> post on LessWrong
              ,
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://tm.kehrenberg.net/a/type-theory1/"} post on personal blog
          %blockquote
            %p “An example of a use case for non-binary sum types is the definition of mathematical “data structures” like a ring or a group. These data structures are usually defined as…”
